1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:03,840
The huge revelation here and what they hammer home in the class is that

2
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:07,920
RE latency is a pre-fill problem, not a retrieval problem.

3
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:08,460
Yes.

4
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:12,600
OK, let's unpack this because to understand this, we have to look at the two distinct

5
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:16,280
phases of how a large language model generates text.

6
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:18,560
The pre-fill phase and the decode phase.

7
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:20,280
That's tear into the pre-fill phase first.

8
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:25,200
When a user sends a request to a RE system, the prompt isn't just, you know,

9
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:26,960
what is our Q3 strategy?

10
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:27,460
Right.

11
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:31,920
The system intercepts that question, searches the database, finds 50 pages of

12
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:36,520
relevant PDFs and cramps all 50 pages into the prompt alongside the question.

13
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:41,400
And when that massive prompt arrives at the LLM, the model must read the entire

14
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:44,040
input before it can generate a single word of the answer.

15
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:44,540
Right.

16
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:45,160
It doesn't just skim it.

17
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:46,680
No, this is the pre-fill phase.

18
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:51,400
The model processes every single token, every piece of every word simultaneously

19
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:52,280
through the neural network.

20
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:56,120
It does this all in parallel, which means the GPUs are just grinding at absolute

21
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:57,160
maximum capacity.

22
00:00:57,160 --> 00:00:58,800
It is entirely compute bound.

23
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:02,560
And it scales quadratically because of the attention mechanism.

24
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:03,640
Oh, this is the kicker.

25
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:05,520
Explain the quadratic scaling.

26
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:10,120
So in a transformer model, every token has to look at every other token to

27
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:11,320
understand the context.

28
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:15,240
If you double the length of the prompt, you don't double the computational

29
00:01:15,240 --> 00:01:16,760
work, you actually quadruple it.

30
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:17,380
Wow.

31
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:17,900
Yeah.

32
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:21,880
The GPU is doing billions of matrix multiplications all at once.

33
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,720
And the goal of all this crazy math is to generate the key value cache.

34
00:01:27,160 --> 00:01:28,280
The KV cache.

35
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:31,680
We really should explain this because it's the secret to how these models

36
00:01:31,680 --> 00:01:32,640
remember the prompt.

37
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:33,260
Yeah.

38
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:37,520
Think of the KV cache as the mathematical summary of the entire prompt as the model

39
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:42,840
processes those 50 pages, it calculates the keys and values for every single word,

40
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:46,840
which is essentially plotting out how each word relates to the others in

41
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:48,040
multi-dimensional space.

42
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:48,680
Exactly.

43
00:01:48,680 --> 00:01:53,480
It stores this massive mathematical map in the GPU's memory so that it doesn't

44
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:55,400
have to reread the 50 pages ever again.

45
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:55,980
Okay.

46
00:01:55,720 --> 00:01:57,160
So the pre-fill phase is complete.

47
00:01:57,400 --> 00:01:58,680
The compute was super heavy.

48
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:00,200
The KV cache is built.

49
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:04,120
Now we enter the decode phase where the model actually starts talking back to you.

50
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:06,600
It generates the first word of the answer.

51
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:10,440
But this phase operates under completely different laws of physics, doesn't it?

52
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:11,520
Completely different.

53
00:02:11,520 --> 00:02:14,120
The decode phase is not compute bound at all.

54
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:14,840
Wait, really?

55
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:15,420
Yeah.

56
00:02:15,360 --> 00:02:16,800
It is memory bandwidth bound.

57
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:21,000
The actual GPU compute cores are barely breaking a sweat.

58
00:02:21,320 --> 00:02:22,160
Why is that?

59
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:28,040
Because the KV cache already exists to generate the next word, the model doesn't

60
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:29,120
look at the prompt again.

61
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:33,800
It just takes the newly generated word, calculates its specific key in value and

62
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,120
appends it to the cache.

63
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,600
Ah, so the arithmetic is incredibly light.

64
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:38,200
Very light.

65
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:40,240
But here is the massive traffic jam.

66
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:40,780
Right.

67
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:45,120
To calculate that one new word, the GPU still has to stream the entire neural

68
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:46,120
network's weights.

69
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:51,400
Let's say 14 gigabytes of data for a medium size model from the memory chips

70
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:55,000
into the processing cores for every single word for every single word.

71
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:59,280
You are moving 14 gigabytes of data across the motherboard just to produce a

72
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:00,400
fraction of a syllable.

73
00:03:00,440 --> 00:03:01,840
That is insane.

74
00:03:01,880 --> 00:03:06,040
So the GPU cores are literally sitting idle for milliseconds at a time, just

75
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:08,040
waiting for the data to arrive from the memory.

76
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:12,120
Yeah, I want to use an analogy here to cement this because it's so counter

77
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:14,200
intuitive to how we normally think about computers.

78
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:14,720
Go for it.

79
00:03:15,080 --> 00:03:18,760
The pre-fill phase is like sitting down to read an entire encyclopedia.

80
00:03:18,800 --> 00:03:19,300
Right.

81
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:20,960
It requires massive brain power.

82
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:24,880
You were concentrating incredibly hard, but you process the whole thing in one

83
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:25,640
giant sitting.

84
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:26,180
Right.

85
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:30,680
The decode phase is like writing the sequel to that encyclopedia, one single

86
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:31,400
word at a time.

87
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:31,940
Okay.

88
00:03:31,960 --> 00:03:35,560
But for every single word you write, you are forced to walk all the way

89
00:03:35,560 --> 00:03:39,720
across town to the library, fetch the original encyclopedia, carry it back to your

90
00:03:39,720 --> 00:03:42,600
desk, write your one word, and then return the book.

91
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:43,200
Yes.

92
00:03:43,360 --> 00:03:47,440
And you have to do that walk across town for the next word and the next.

93
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:49,240
That is a perfect analogy.

94
00:03:49,480 --> 00:03:52,400
The walking across town is the memory bandwidth bottleneck.

95
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:57,520
This asymmetry explains a fundamental rule of LLM ups that a lot of people miss.

96
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:03,160
Batching multiple user requests together helps the decode phase immensely, but does

97
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:04,880
almost nothing for the pre-fill phase.

98
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:05,660
Right.

99
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:09,480
Because if you are walking to the library anyway, you might as well fetch the

100
00:04:09,480 --> 00:04:13,720
information needed to write 64 different words for 54 different users all at once.

101
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:15,400
You share the cost of the commute.

102
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:16,120
Exactly.

103
00:04:16,120 --> 00:04:19,760
But during pre-fill, your brain is already maxed out reading that first

104
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:24,640
encyclopedia, adding a second users encyclopedia at the same time will just

105
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:25,560
crash your brain.

106
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:30,160
Therefore, when a rag application is slow, it isn't the database search.

107
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:35,760
It is the model choking on the compute heavy pre-fill phase as it tries to

108
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:40,640
quadratically process the 50 pages of PDFs you just dumped into its prompt.

109
00:04:41,080 --> 00:04:42,200
So how do we fix it?

110
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:42,900
Yeah.

111
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:44,880
Because we can't just stop feeding the model context.

112
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:45,380
No.

113
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:46,160
You need the context.

114
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:46,660
Right.

115
00:04:46,400 --> 00:04:51,120
So the master class tackles this by introducing bleeding edge inference engines

116
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:55,880
and a technique called speculative decoding, specifically focusing on a

117
00:04:55,880 --> 00:04:57,400
technology called D flash.

118
00:04:57,640 --> 00:05:01,280
Speculative decoding is I think one of the most brilliant architectural

119
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:02,880
workarounds we've seen in the last year.

120
00:05:03,160 --> 00:05:08,080
We know the massive 14 gigabyte model is slow at generating text because

121
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:09,760
fetching the weights takes so long.

122
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:14,320
So instead of asking the massive model to generate the text one word at a

123
00:05:14,320 --> 00:05:20,400
time, we introduce a tiny secondary draft model, a much smaller model that

124
00:05:20,400 --> 00:05:22,360
can hold its entire weight in the fast cache.

125
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:23,080
Exactly.

126
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:29,160
This tiny draft model races ahead and guesses the next say 15 tokens almost instantly.

127
00:05:29,280 --> 00:05:29,780
Okay.

128
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:30,280
But it's small.

129
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:32,000
So the guesses might not be perfectly accurate.

130
00:05:32,040 --> 00:05:32,540
Right.

131
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:33,680
But they are fast.

132
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:39,000
Then it hands those 15 guest tokens to the massive slow model.

133
00:05:39,080 --> 00:05:40,280
And here's the magic trick.

134
00:05:40,880 --> 00:05:43,840
The massive model doesn't have to evaluate them one by one.

135
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:49,240
It evaluates all 15 tokens simultaneously because verifying text acts exactly like a

136
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:49,880
pre-fill step.

137
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:50,420
Yes.

138
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:51,520
It does it in parallel.

139
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:56,040
If the tiny model just perfectly, you just generated 15 tokens in the time it

140
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:57,200
usually takes to generate one.

141
00:05:57,240 --> 00:05:58,520
That is a massive speed up.

142
00:05:58,600 --> 00:06:03,120
And even if the tiny model gets wrong on say the fifth token, the massive model

143
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:07,240
corrects it, throws away the rest of the guesses and you still gained four perfectly

144
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:08,920
generated tokens essentially for free.

145
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:09,960
It's astonishing.

146
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:12,240
And D flash takes this a step further, doesn't it?

147
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:12,740
It does.

148
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,320
It isn't just a standard draft model.

149
00:06:14,320 --> 00:06:18,480
It's a lightweight block diffusion model designed specifically for this drafting

150
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:19,080
process.

151
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:22,400
It enables highly efficient parallel drafting.

152
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:27,320
And the curriculum shows how to wire D flash directly into modern serving

153
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:33,200
engines like VLLM, SG Lang, and even MLX for developers running local models

154
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:36,480
on Apple Silicon, which is huge for local development.

155
00:06:36,520 --> 00:06:39,640
But you know, having a lightning fast model is only half the battle.

156
00:06:39,720 --> 00:06:40,220
Right.

157
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:43,720
If you are building an actual AI agent, you don't just need one model.

158
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:47,720
You need an entire ecosystem of specialized models working together, which

159
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:49,960
introduces just a horrific DevOps nightmare.

160
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:50,800
Oh, the worst.

161
00:06:51,320 --> 00:06:54,680
Let's look at what a real world autonomous agent actually has to do.

162
00:06:55,160 --> 00:06:58,520
It needs to search internal databases, which requires an embedding model.

163
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:02,840
It needs to read scanned PDFs, which requires a specialized document

164
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:04,120
parsing OCR model.

165
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:08,760
It needs to output data in strict JSON formats, requiring an extraction model.

166
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:11,720
It needs safety guardrails, requiring a moderation model.

167
00:07:11,760 --> 00:07:15,080
And it needs the main heavy duty LOM for its reasoning loop.

168
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:19,440
Traditionally, a backend engineer would have to provision five different cloud

169
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:25,000
servers, manage five different endpoints, handle scaling and load balancing for each

